What is TCL?
..................
TCL (Transaction Control Language) is used to manage transactions in a database.
............................................
A transaction is a group of SQL statements executed as a single unit.

TCL Commands
......................
COMMIT
ROLLBACK
SAVEPOINT
START TRANSACTION

COMMIT	                                   ROLLBACK
................................................................

Saves all changes permanently	            Undoes changes
Cannot be undone	                        Can be used before COMMIT
Ends the transaction	                    Restores previous state


What is a SAVEPOINT?
......................
A SAVEPOINT creates a checkpoint inside a transaction. You can roll back to that checkpoint without undoing the entire transaction.

4. Can we roll back after COMMIT?

No.

5. What happens if the database crashes before COMMIT?
The transaction is rolled back automatically.
This behavior is guaranteed by the Atomicity and Durability properties of ACID.


What is START TRANSACTION?

START TRANSACTION marks the beginning of a transaction.
Everything between START TRANSACTION and COMMIT (or ROLLBACK) belongs to the same transaction.


7. Why are transactions important in banking applications?

Transactions ensure that money is transferred correctly.

Example

Transfer $1,000 from Account A to Account B.


A - Atomicity
........................
Either all operations succeed or none do.

Example:
.....................
Debit $1,000
Credit $1,000

If the credit fails, the debit is also undone

C - Consistency
..................
The database remains in a valid state.

Example:

Before transfer:

A = $10,000
B = $5,000

Total = $15,000

After transfer:

A = $9,000
B = $6,000

Total remains $15,000

I - Isolation
.......................
Multiple transactions do not interfere with each other.

Example:
.........
Two customers withdrawing money simultaneously should not corrupt the account balance.


D - Durability
........................
After COMMIT, changes are permanent even if the database crashes.

Q Can a transaction have multiple SAVEPOINTs?

Yes.


10. What is the difference between ROLLBACK and ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT?
......................................
ROLLBACK	                         ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT
......................................
Undoes the entire transaction	                           Undoes only to a specific savepoint
Returns to the state before START TRANSACTION	           Returns to the specified savepoint
Removes all uncommitted changes                          	Preserves changes made before the savepoint


